<Project xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <!--
    THE .NET SDK'S SECURITY ANALYSERS, FOR THE TWO OLD-STYLE PROJECTS - OutlookAI.csproj and
    PolicyWriter\OutlookAI.PolicyWriter.csproj, which both import this file. Decided by the
    maintainer 2026-10-03 (Q125 (b)): with the GitHub CI went CodeQL's scan, so the builds run the
    security analysers that ship with the .NET SDK instead. The MCP server's projects get the same
    rules, at the same level, from McpServer\Directory.Build.props; this is the add-in's way to them.

    WHY AN SDK IMPORT AND NOT A PACKAGE. The analysers are part of the .NET SDK
    (<sdk>\Sdks\Microsoft.NET.Sdk\analyzers), which is where an SDK-style project takes them from.
    These two projects are not SDK-style, and both build scripts run MSBuild with no restore step
    (PolicyWriter's project file says why), so a PackageReference would never resolve - and a package
    is a download this needs no more than the Dependencies rule wants it. The import at the bottom
    asks Visual Studio's MSBuild to resolve Microsoft.NET.Sdk - the SDK the MCP server builds with,
    found the same way - and imports that SDK's own analyser targets, so these projects run the
    SDK's analysers from the SDK's own rule-set files. Measured 2026-10-04: Visual Studio 18.10.3
    (Roslyn 5.10) with SDK 10.0.401, on a scratch project first and then on the add-in itself.

    THE LEVEL, 10.0-all (the same in McpServer\Directory.Build.props): every rule in the Security
    category that .NET 10's analysers ship, and only that category.
      * all, not the default or "recommended": the default runs almost none of them, and
        "recommended" leaves out the injection, deserialisation and DLL-search rules (CA2100,
        CA3001-CA3012, CA2300-CA2362, CA5392), the ones a code scan exists for. "all" found 78
        sites in the MCP server's projects when it was switched on - 71 of them one rule, CA5392 -
        and one more in the add-in: a list one person can triage, not a flood.
      * Security only: "all" in every category is thousands of style and design findings, and it
        would bury the few that matter. That is not what Q125 asked for.
      * pinned at 10.0, not "latest": an SDK update must not change the rule set under a build
        that passed. A newer level is a deliberate edit of this number and of the props file.
    Every finding is an ERROR. These two projects do not otherwise treat warnings as errors, and a
    finding that only warns in a scripted build is one nobody reads - so
    CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors makes the SDK pick its "_warnaserror" rule-set files.
    A real finding is fixed. A false positive is suppressed AT ITS SITE, with
    [SuppressMessage("Security", "CAxxxx:...", Justification = "<why it is safe>")] - never here,
    never by rule, and never without the reason.

    Imported AFTER Microsoft.CSharp.targets: the SDK's file reads $(Language), which that sets.
    Visual Studio's own builds read it too, so F5 shows the same findings.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <AnalysisLevelSecurity>10.0-all</AnalysisLevelSecurity>
    <CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors>true</CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="..\targets\Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Analyzers.targets" Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk" />
</Project>
